{
  "name": "Arpeggio",
  "notes": "A note jumps between positions and its COLOUR comes from where it landed, not from anything about the note. That is `index: \"position\"` used as a keyboard -- the palette is laid once along the strip and a line stepping through it plays it like a scale. The line carries no colour of its own; it reveals one already there, which is the distinction `showcase/05-ladder` draws between Position and U, put to work.\n\nThe steps are a `sample_hold` at a small non-zero `slew`: 0.03 s is about six frames at 200 FPS, fast enough to still read as a step and slow enough that the eye follows it.\n\nTwo chains stack. The draw drives `M2.rate` -- a second, slower sequencer whose tempo is set by the last note. Note what LIFE-698 means here: `LfoSignal::seed` is not in the document, so both `sample_hold` sources draw the IDENTICAL sequence. They are not two independent players. Because the second runs at a rate the first is setting, it plays the same notes at a different and continually changing speed, which is a canon rather than a counterpoint -- deliberate here, and worth knowing before copying this shape somewhere it would be wrong. And `M3` drives `M0.rate`, walking the arpeggio's tempo through a factor of four on a slow noise clock, which is the thing a fixed sequencer cannot do. The two voices run OPPOSITE directions from the same source, so they cross in the middle of the strip on beats nobody scheduled. Split complement: cerise and violet 25 degrees apart with acid lime 200 away, and the lime lives at one end of the keyboard so only the highest notes reach it.",
